\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Jacobian and covariance in orthonormal charts}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Choose orthonormal charts #{C:M\to\mathbb R^n} and
#{C':M'\to\mathbb R^n}. Write
#{A=C'TC^{-1}}, #{R=CQC^{-1}} and #{R'=C'Q'(C')^{-1}}.
The identities of \ref{fdip-003M} are
##{A^TR'A=R,\qquad (R')^{-1}=AR^{-1}A^T,\qquad J(T)=|\det A|.}
Thus the transpose formula #{(Q')^{-1}=TQ^{-1}T^T} is the
same identity when #{T,Q,Q'} denote their arrays in these charts.
The transported reference pairing has array #{A^{-T}A^{-1}}.
Independently ordering the two orthonormal frames may change the
signed determinant, but not its absolute value.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
supports this covariance congruence with mean and shift zero, supplied
linear map #{A}, and covariance #{R^{-1}}. It also allows rectangular
maps. The declaration does not assert invertibility, endpoint
preservation or a retained square. The density and volume conventions
are \citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical} and
\citet{Exercises 1.2.20-22, p. 41}{tao2011measure}.}
